  </handout>
  
    <handout>
      <title>Graph Coloring</title>
      <p>
        <alert>Peer instruction questions 1<ndash/>3.</alert>
      </p>
      <p>
        Let <m>\bfG=(V,E)</m> be a graph. Then <m>\phi\colon V\to C</m> is a proper coloring of <m>\bfG</m> if
        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\phi</m> is one-to-one
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>xy\in E</m> implies <m>\phi(x)\neq \phi(y)</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>\phi</m> uses as few colors from <m>C</m> as possible
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              None of the above
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>

      <p>
        Let <m>\bfG=(V,E)</m> be a graph and <m>\phi\colon V\to C</m> be a proper coloring of <m>\bfG</m>. Let <m>S</m> be all vertices colored <m>1\in C</m>. How many edges does the subgraph of <m>\bfG</m> induced by <m>S</m> contain?
        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>
              0
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              1
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>|S|</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              Any number is possible.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <p>
        What is the chromatic number of the complete graph on <m>n</m> vertices <m>\bfK_{n}</m>?
        <ol marker="A.">
          <li>
            <p>
              <m>n-1</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>n</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              <m>n+1</m>
            </p>
          </li>

          <li>
            <p>
              There is no fixed formula depending on <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      <sidebyside widths="60% 40%">
      <image>
        <shortdescription>A graph with 11 vertices</shortdescription>
        <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="mycielski-bad-coloring">
          <diagram dimensions="(400,300)" margins="20">
            <coordinates bbox="(0,-0.3,4.2,2)">
              <network labels="yes">
                <node at="1" p="(2,2)" edges="[2,3,4,5,6]"><m>z</m></node>
                <node at="2" p="(0,1)" edges="[8,11]"><m>y_1</m></node>
                <node at="3" p="(1,1)" edges="[7,9]"><m>y_2</m></node>
                <node at="4" p="(2,1)" edges="[8,10]"><m>y_3</m></node>
                <node at="5" p="(3,1)" edges="[9,11]"><m>y_4</m></node>
                <node at="6" p="(4,1)" edges="[7,10]"><m>y_5</m></node>
                <node at="7" p="(0,-0.15)" edges="[8]"><m>x_1</m></node>
                <node at="8" p="(1,0)" edges="[9]"><m>x_2</m></node>
                <node at="9" p="(2,0)" edges="[10]"><m>x_3</m></node>
                <node at="10" p="(3,0)" edges="[11]"><m>x_4</m></node>
                <node at="11" p="(4,-0.15)"><m>x_5</m></node>
                <edge vertices="(7,11)"/>
              </network>
              <label p="(1.87,2)" alignment="w"><m>1</m></label>
              <label p="(0.2,1)" alignment="e"><m>2</m></label>
              <label p="(1.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>3</m></label>
              <label p="(2.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>4</m></label>
              <label p="(3.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>2</m></label>
              <label p="(4.1,1)" alignment="e"><m>5</m></label>
              <label p="(0,-.25)" alignment="s"><m>4</m></label>
              <label p="(1.1,0)" alignment="ne"><m>1</m></label>
              <label p="(2.1,0)" alignment="ne"><m>5</m></label>
              <label p="(2.9,0)" alignment="nw"><m>3</m></label>
              <label p="(4,-0.25)" alignment="s"><m>1</m></label>
            </coordinates>
          </diagram>
        </prefigure>
      </image>
      <p><nbsp/></p>
    </sidebyside>
<definition workspace="4cm">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A graph is <term>bipartite</term> provided that its chromatic number is <m>2</m>.
    </p>
  </statement>
</definition>

<theorem workspace="4cm">
  <statement>
    <p>
      A graph is bipartite if and only if it does not contain any odd cycles.
    </p>
  </statement>
</theorem>  
<p>
  <alert>Peer instruction question 4.</alert>
</p>
